2}}\\).\n\nWe start by examining the boundary values of \\(x\\), since the minimum might occur at the endpoints or critical points within the interval \\([-1, 1]\\).\n\n1. **Evaluate at \\(x = 1\\):**\n   \\[\n   f(1) = \\sqrt{58 - 42 \\cdot 1} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - 1^2}} = \\sqrt{58 - 42} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\cdot 0} = \\sqrt{16} + \\sqrt{149} = 4 + \\sqrt{149}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\sqrt{149} \\approx 12.21\\), \\(f(1) \\approx 4 + 12.21 = 16.21\\).\n\n2. **Evaluate at \\(x = -1\\):**\n   \\[\n   f(-1) = \\sqrt{58 - 42 \\cdot (-1)} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - (-1)^2}} = \\sqrt{58 + 42} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\cdot 0} = \\sqrt{100} + \\sqrt{149} = 10 + \\sqrt{149}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\sqrt{149} \\approx 12.21\\), \\(f(-1) \\approx 10 + 12.21 = 22.21\\).\n\nNext, we need to check if there is a minimum value within the interval \\([-1, 1]\\). To do this, we can consider the possibility of a critical point by differentiating \\(f(x)\\) and setting the derivative to zero, but this can be quite complex. Instead, we can use a substitution or a known result to simplify the problem.\n\nNotice that the expression \\(\\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - x^2}}\\) is minimized when \\(\\sqrt{1 - x^2}\\) is maximized, which happens when \\(x = 0\\).\n\n3. **Evaluate at \\(x = 0\\):**\n   \\[\n   f(0) = \\sqrt{58 - 42 \\cdot 0} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - 0^2}} = \\sqrt{58} + \\sqrt{149 - 140} = \\sqrt{58} + \\sqrt{9} = \\sqrt{58} + 3\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\sqrt{58} \\approx 7.62\\), \\(f(0) \\approx 7.62 + 3 = 10.62\\).\n\nBy comparing the values at the endpoints and at \\(x = 0\\), we see that the minimum value is at \\(x = 0\\).\n\nThus, the minimum possible value of the expression is \\(\\boxed{10.62}\\). However, since we are asked to provide the exact value, we should use the exact form \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{58} + 3}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{58} + 3}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
